<example xml:id="example-cardioid-centroid">
<title>Centroid of a Cardioid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the centroid of the object that has the shape
<m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> and density <m>\delta = 2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Centroid of a Cardioid.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid-video">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> with constant density: symmetry
places the centroid on the <m>x</m>-axis, and the polar moment integrals
locate it. Use the animation to check your value.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-cardioid-centroid"
       source="videos/cardioid_centroid_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
A cardioid symmetric about the x axis with shaded interior.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cardioid-centroid">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="10">
<definition>c(t) = ((1 + cos(t))*cos(t), (1 + cos(t))*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.9,-1.55,2.65,1.55)">
<axes at="cd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cd-cardioid" function="c" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                  closed="yes" fill="#9ecfff" stroke="blue"
                  thickness="2.5"/>
<label at="cd-curve-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(1.55,-1.05)" alignment="se"
       color="blue">
<m>r=1+\cos(\theta)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A cardioid, the heart-shaped curve r equals 1 plus cosine theta, drawn in blue with its interior shaded light blue. It has a cusp at the origin and its widest point at x equals 2.">
  <annotation ref="cd-cardioid" text="The cardioid r equals 1 plus cosine theta, a closed heart-shaped curve with a cusp at the origin, shaded light blue."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-mass-3d-object">
<title>Finding the Center of Mass of a 3D Object*</title>

<p>
Everything so far took place in the plane. For point masses in space,
with <m>\mathbf{r}_i = (x_i, y_i, z_i)</m> in
<xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/>, the distances are measured from the
coordinate planes instead of the coordinate axes. The
<term>first moments about the coordinate planes</term> are
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
M_{yz} \amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, x_i,
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-first-moments-points-3d" number="yes">
M_{xz} \amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, y_i,
</mrow>
<mrow>
M_{xy} \amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, z_i
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
(<m>M_{yz}</m> uses the signed distance <m>x_i</m> from the
<m>yz</m>-plane, and so on), and the center of mass
<m>\bar{\mathbf{r}} = (\bar{x}, \bar{y}, \bar{z})</m> is
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\bar{x} \amp = \frac{M_{yz}}{M},
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-com-points-3d" number="yes">
\bar{y} \amp = \frac{M_{xz}}{M},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\bar{z} \amp = \frac{M_{xy}}{M}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Now let a solid object occupy a region <m>D</m> in space, with
density <m>\delta(x,y,z)</m> (mass per unit volume) at the point
<m>(x,y,z)</m>. The plan is the same as for a plate, one dimension up:
we cut the solid into small boxes and treat each box as a point mass.
Partition space into boxes by the planes <m>x = x_0, \dots, x_n</m>,
<m>y = y_0, \dots, y_m</m>, and <m>z = z_0, \dots, z_p</m>. The box in
position <m>i</m> along the <m>x</m>-direction, <m>j</m> along the
<m>y</m>-direction, and <m>k</m> along the <m>z</m>-direction has the
dimensions <m>\Delta x_i</m>, <m>\Delta y_j</m>, <m>\Delta z_k</m> and the
volume <m>\Delta V_{ijk} = \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k</m>; if
it meets <m>D</m>, we pick a point <m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m> in it, as in
<xref ref="fig-mass-element-tikz"/>. Write down the approximate mass
<m>\Delta m_{ijk}</m> of this small box, treat the boxes as a system of
point masses, and use <xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/> and
<xref ref="eq-first-moments-points-3d"/> to write the mass and the first
moments of the solid as triple sums; then let the boxes shrink,
<m>n, m, p \to \infty</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the mass and the first moments of the solid as triple sums and then as triple integrals.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-mass-3d">
<title>Mass of a Solid</title>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>
\amp M = \lim_{n,m,p \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{p}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-mass-3d" number="yes">
\amp = \iiint_D \delta(x,y,z)\, dV.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Watch the construction unfold in <xref ref="fig-mass-element-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-mass-element-video">
<caption>
Partitioning a solid <m>D</m> into small boxes: each box carries the
mass <q>density times volume</q>, and the Riemann sum of these masses
converges to the triple integral of the density.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-mass-element"
       source="videos/mass_element_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-mass-element-tikz">
<caption>
A solid region <m>D</m> with a small box around the point
<m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m>. The box has the volume <m>\Delta V_{ijk}</m> and
the mass <m>\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta V_{ijk}</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-mass-element-tikz" width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A potato-shaped solid in space with a small highlighted box inside it.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent potato-shaped solid labeled <m>D</m> floats above the
<m>xy</m>-plane in a three-dimensional coordinate system. A small
purple box inside the solid marks the point <m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m>,
and a leader line labels its mass
<m>\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta V_{ijk}</m>.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \tikzset{lbl/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.82, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt, rounded corners=2pt}}
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=12cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis equal image,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-2.9, xmax=2.9, ymin=-2.9, ymax=2.9, zmin=0, zmax=4.1,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- the solid D: a deformed (potato-like) ellipsoid ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softg}{color=(oigreen!14!white) color=(oigreen!48!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:180, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({(2+0.35*sin(2*x))*cos(x)*sin(y)},
       {(1.35+0.2*cos(2*x))*sin(x)*sin(y)},
       {0.95*cos(y)+2.3});
    % ---- small box (mass element) inside the solid ----
    % cube centered near (1.05, 0.55, 2.6) with half-side 0.21
    \def\cx{1.05}\def\cy{0.55}\def\cz{2.60}\def\hs{0.21}
    % top face
    \fill[oipurple!70, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % front face (toward viewer, +x side)
    \fill[oipurple!45, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % side face (+y side)
    \fill[oipurple!30, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[lbl, font=\large] at (axis cs:-1.7,-0.4,3.1) {$D$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- (axis cs:1.6,2.0,3.7);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.6,2.0,3.7)
      {$\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i,y_j,z_k)\,\Delta V_{ijk}$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) -- (axis cs:1.2,2.3,1.9);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.2,2.3,1.9)
      {$(x_i, y_j, z_k)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
In the same way, the first moments become the triple integrals
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
M_{yz} \amp = \iiint_D x\, \delta\, dV,
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-first-moments-3d" number="yes">
M_{xz} \amp = \iiint_D y\, \delta\, dV,
</mrow>
<mrow>
M_{xy} \amp = \iiint_D z\, \delta\, dV,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
the moments about the <m>yz</m>-, <m>xz</m>-, and <m>xy</m>-planes, and
the center of mass <m>(\bar{x}, \bar{y}, \bar{z})</m> of the solid is
the point whose coordinates are the moments divided by the total mass:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\bar{x} \amp = \frac{M_{yz}}{M},
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-com-3d" number="yes">
\bar{y} \amp = \frac{M_{xz}}{M},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\bar{z} \amp = \frac{M_{xy}}{M}.
</mrow>
</md>

<example xml:id="example-paraboloid-com">
<title>Center of Mass of a Solid Bounded by a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the center of mass of a solid of constant density <m>\delta</m>,
which is bounded by the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> (in the plane
<m>z = 0</m>) and the paraboloid <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Center of Mass of a Solid Bounded by a Paraboloid.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-com-video">
<caption>
The solid bounded above by <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> and below by the
disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>. By symmetry the center of mass lies on the
<m>z</m>-axis; use the animation to check its height.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-paraboloid-com"
       source="videos/paraboloid_com_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-com-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of constant density bounded by the paraboloid
<m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> and the disk <m>R</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>
in the plane <m>z = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-paraboloid-com-tikz" width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A dome-shaped solid over a disk.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The paraboloid <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> forms a translucent green dome
over the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, labeled
<m>R</m>. The dashed <m>z</m>-axis passes through the apex at height 4.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \tikzset{lbl/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.82, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt, rounded corners=2pt}}
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oimagenta}{RGB}{204,0,119}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis equal image,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-3, xmax=4.4, ymin=-3, ymax=3, zmin=0, zmax=4.6,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- base disk R ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=flat,
              color=black!12, opacity=0.4, fill opacity=0.4,
              domain=0:360, samples=37,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {0});
    % ---- paraboloid surface ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softg}{color=(oigreen!14!white) color=(oigreen!48!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=61,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {4 - y^2});
    % light parallels for shape cues
    \foreach \rr in {0.5,1,1.5}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:360,
          samples=73, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({\rr*cos(x)}, {\rr*sin(x)}, {4 - \rr*\rr});}\tmp}
    % light meridians for shape cues
    \foreach \uu in {0,45,...,315}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:2,
          samples=25, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({x*cos(\uu)}, {x*sin(\uu)}, {4 - x^2});}\tmp}
    % ---- boundary circle x^2 + y^2 = 4 ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.6pt, oiblue, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({2*cos(x)}, {2*sin(x)}, {0});
    % ---- dashed axis of symmetry through the solid ----
    \draw[dashed, black!60, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,4);
    % ---- labels ----
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{1.35*cos(35)},{1.35*sin(35)},{4-1.35^2}) -- (axis cs:1.9,2.5,3.4);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.9,2.5,3.4)
      {$z = 4 - x^2 - y^2$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{2*cos(320)},{2*sin(320)},0) -- (axis cs:1.4,-2.75,0.75);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=east] at (axis cs:1.4,-2.75,0.75)
      {$x^2 + y^2 = 4$};
    \node[lbl, font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis cs:2.0,1.3,0) {$R$};
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize] at (axis cs:-0.3,-0.25,0) {$0$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-mass-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The first problem is about point masses, where everything can be done by
hand in a line or two; the second revisits the region of
<xref ref="example-centroid-between-curves"/> with two densities that are
not constant.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-mass-three-points">
<title>Three point masses</title>
<statement>
<p>
Masses of <m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>3</m> kilograms sit at the points
<m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(4,0)</m>, and <m>(0,4)</m>, respectively. Find the
center of mass of the system. Where would it be if the three masses were
equal?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,6.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the center of mass of the three point masses, and of three equal masses.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-mass-two-densities">
<title>The same plate, two densities</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the center of mass of the region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> and
<m>y = x^2</m>, assuming that the density function is
</p>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>\delta_1(x,y) = xy</m>;</p></li>
<li><p><m>\delta_2(x,y) = y</m>.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Question:</em> Do your answers in parts A and B match your intuition?
If so, justify your answer in each case. (For comparison, find the
centroid of this region first, in
<xref ref="example-centroid-between-curves"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,15.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the center of mass with each of the two densities, and the comparison with intuition.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
